Round Robin Scheduling

Definition
Round Robin is a preemptive CPU scheduling algorithm.
Each process is assigned a fixed time quantum.
Processes are executed in a circular (cyclic) order.

Characteristics 
Preemptive in nature: Running process is interrupted after time quantum.
Time quantum based scheduling: CPU is allocated for a fixed time slice to each process.
Fair allocation of CPU: Every process gets equal opportunity, avoiding starvation.
Uses circular queue: Processes are scheduled in FIFO cyclic order.
High context switching overhead: Frequent switching reduces efficiency.

Working Principle 
Processes are placed in a ready queue.
CPU selects the first process.
Process executes for time quantum.
If incomplete → moved to end of queue.
If complete → removed from system.
Next process is selected.
Repeat until all processes finish.

Priority Scheduling

Definition
Priority Scheduling is a CPU scheduling algorithm.
Each process is assigned a priority value.
Process with highest priority executes first.

Characteristics 
Priority-based execution: CPU is allocated based on priority levels.
Preemptive or non-preemptive : Can interrupt running process (preemptive case).
Flexible scheduling method: Priorities can be static or dynamic.
Starvation problem: Low priority processes may wait indefinitely.
Aging technique used: Gradually increases priority to prevent starvation.

Working Principle (
Assign priority to each process.
Processes enter the ready queue.
CPU selects highest priority process.
Executes the process.
In preemptive case → higher priority can interrupt.
After completion → next highest priority selected.
Repeat until all processes are executed.
